how does LinkedIn work LinkedIn's recent  quarterly earnings taught 473 million  dollars up from 25 million five years  ago the 11 year old company claims more  than 300 million members worldwide a  third of them here in the US it makes  money in three different ways one job  seekers upgrading to premium  subscriptions unlike other social  networks people pay for special access  subscribers COFF up twenty to fifty  dollars a month to contact recruiters  directly about jobs they're interested  in and make sure their resumes rise to  the top of the pile  LinkedIn sees 44,000 job applications  passed through its site every day so  these advantages may help candidates  stand out to companies selling to other  companies and to users LinkedIn's no  Facebook or Google when it comes to ad  sales but the company's information does  allow for highly targeted marketing  campaigns ad sales brought in 326  million dollars last year accounting for  more than a fit of the company's 2013  revenues three recruiters buying access  to members data and caring for their  firm to get noticed  recruitment professionals pay thousands  of dollars each month to access  LinkedIn's databases once upon a time  only job seekers posted resumes online  but these days through LinkedIn top  companies can access potentially  suitable candidates who may not even be  looking for a new job this business area  known as talent solutions is the  company's golden goose bringing more  than half of Lincoln's revenues so it's  no surprise that the company hopes to  entice the rest of the world's three  billion professionals to connect so that  they too can be found online
 

